> the integral of x^2 from 0 to pi
  ucas: it(x) = x^2
  ucas: integrate it from 0 to pi
∫ x^2 dx
  = x^3/3 + C
  check: d/dx of it equals the integrand at 401 points of [0, 3.14159] (max difference 8.9e-16)
  from 0 to 3.14159: F(3.14159) - F(0) = 10.3354255600999
  numeric (adaptive Gauss-Kronrod 7-15, 15 evaluations): 10.3354255600999, estimated error 0
  the two agree to 0
> the integral of sin x from 0 to pi
  ucas: it(x) = sin(x)
  ucas: integrate it from 0 to pi
∫ sin(x) dx
  = -cos(x) + C
  check: d/dx of it equals the integrand at 401 points of [0, 3.14159] (max difference 0)
  from 0 to 3.14159: F(3.14159) - F(0) = 2
  numeric (adaptive Gauss-Kronrod 7-15, 15 evaluations): 2, estimated error 2.2e-14
  the two agree to 0
> the integral of 1/x from 1 to e
  ucas: it(x) = 1/x
  ucas: integrate it from 1 to e
∫ 1/x dx
  = log(abs(x)) + C
  check: d/dx of it equals the integrand at 401 points of [1, 2.71828] (max difference 0)
  from 1 to 2.71828: F(2.71828) - F(1) = 1
  numeric (adaptive Gauss-Kronrod 7-15, 15 evaluations): 1, estimated error 1.1e-14
  the two agree to 0
> antiderivative of x^2
  ucas: it(x) = x^2
  ucas: antiderivative it
∫ x^2 dx
  = x^3/3 + C
  check: d/dx of it equals the integrand at 401 points of [-10, 10] (max difference 1.4e-14)
> the primitive of cos x
  ucas: it(x) = cos(x)
  ucas: antiderivative it
∫ cos(x) dx
  = sin(x) + C
  check: d/dx of it equals the integrand at 401 points of [-10, 10] (max difference 0)
> the integral of x*sin(x) from 0 to pi
  ucas: it(x) = x*sin(x)
  ucas: integrate it from 0 to pi
∫ x*sin(x) dx
  = sin(x) - x*cos(x) + C
  check: d/dx of it equals the integrand at 401 points of [0, 3.14159] (max difference 2.2e-16)
  from 0 to 3.14159: F(3.14159) - F(0) = 3.14159265358979
  numeric (adaptive Gauss-Kronrod 7-15, 15 evaluations): 3.14159265358979, estimated error 3.5e-14
  the two agree to 8.9e-16
> integrate 1/(1+x^2) from 0 to 1
∫ 1/(x^2 + 1) dx
  = atan(x) + C
  check: d/dx of it equals the integrand at 401 points of [0, 1] (max difference 0)
  from 0 to 1: F(1) - F(0) = 0.785398163397448
  numeric (adaptive Gauss-Kronrod 7-15, 15 evaluations): 0.785398163397448, estimated error 8.6e-15
  the two agree to 1.1e-16
> the integral of sqrt(x) from 0 to 4
  ucas: it(x) = sqrt(x)
  ucas: integrate it from 0 to 4
∫ sqrt(x) dx
  = 2sqrt(x)^3/3 + C
  method: substitution t = sqrt(x)
  check: d/dx of it equals the integrand at 400 points of [0, 4] (max difference 4.4e-16)
  from 0 to 4: F(4) - F(0) = 5.33333333333333
  numeric (adaptive Gauss-Kronrod 7-15, 555 evaluations): 5.33333333333413, estimated error 3.2e-13
  the two agree to 8e-13
> the integral of log x from 1 to e
  note: "log" without a base: read as the natural log, ln, not log base 2 or log base 10 (say "natural log", "log base 2" or "log base 10", or "assume log means base 2")
  ucas: it(x) = log(x)
  ucas: integrate it from 1 to e
∫ log(x) dx
  = x*log(x) - x + C
  check: d/dx of it equals the integrand at 401 points of [1, 2.71828] (max difference 2.2e-16)
  from 1 to 2.71828: F(2.71828) - F(1) = 1
  numeric (adaptive Gauss-Kronrod 7-15, 15 evaluations): 1, estimated error 1.1e-14
  the two agree to 0
